ts are room temperature" is an idealization. I'll structure a friendly, clear answer.
01:07:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and it gets at the difference between a simplified science-class idea and what really happens in a room. The short answer is: **they usually aren’t exactly the same temperature, even when the room has been stable for hours, and even when they are, they can *feel* dramatically different because of how they exchange heat with your skin.** Let’s break it down. --- ### 1. The “perfect equilibrium” assumption rarely holds completely In an idealized physics problem, you assume a closed room with no heat sources, perfectly still air, and enough time for everything to reach thermal equilibrium. Under those conditions, all objects *would* eventually match the air temperature. But real rooms are never perfectly steady: - **Air temperature can drift** (furnace cycling, drafts, sunlight through windows, people entering). - **Some objects generate their own heat** (electronics, incandescent lights, even a pet sleeping on the couch). - **Radiative heat exchange** is happening all the time: a window is colder than the wall in winter, so objects near it radiate heat toward it and stay cooler than objects near an interior wall. Because different materials absorb and release heat at different rates (thermal mass), they lag behind room temperature changes. When the room warms up quickly, a heavy stone tabletop will still be cold from last night, while a sheet of paper catches up almost instantly. --- ### 2. Thermal conductivity makes identical temperatures feel different This is the most common source of confusion. When you touch something, you don’t directly feel its temperature; **you feel how quickly it’s pulling heat out of (or dumping heat into) your skin.** A metal doorknob and a book sitting next to it might both be at exactly 21 °C (70 °F). But metal has high thermal conductivity — it whisks heat away from your warm fingers rapidly, giving a sensation of cold. The book is an insulator; it barely draws heat, so it feels neutral or even slightly warm. That’s why a metal rail feels painfully cold in winter even though it’s the same temperature as the wooden bench beside it. --- ### 3. Temperature isn’t uniform across the room Air itself isn’t one temperature. Warm air rises, so the floor can be 2–3 °C cooler than the ceiling. Objects near the floor (a tile floor, a low shelf) may be colder than those at head height. Add a sunbeam hitting one spot, and you’ve got a hot patch. A curtain near an uninsulated wall might be 15 °C while the interior wall is 20 °C. --- ### 4. Heat capacity and time delays Some objects (a large pot of water, a concrete wall) have a lot of thermal mass. They change temperature slowly. If the room warmed up at 8 AM from a cool night, the water in a glass might still be lagging at 10 AM, while a feather pillow caught up in minutes. So at any given moment, you can find a spread of temperatures across objects even if every one of them is on its way to the same final value. --- ### So to your original thought: Yes, **in the long‑run trend**, everything in a room *aims* to reach the air temperature. But “room temperature” is more of a range than a fixed point, and a combination of heat sources, radiation, conduction delays, and your own perception can easily make it seem like different objects are at different temperatures — and often they truly are, if only by a few degrees.
